const CAPTION = "Two numbers carry the trades labour-shortage thesis. Neither is in the study it gets attributed to.\n\nThe first is a $33 billion annual cost for the plumber shortage. It is not in the LIXIL / John Dunham & Associates work it is cited to. That study's figure is $1.27 billion, and it runs the other way \u2014 the annual saving from adding about 16,400 plumbers (Blocked Pipes, 2024; press release 11 March 2026).\n\nThe second is a $1 trillion annual economic loss, quoted alongside JLL's estimate of 2.1 million unfilled skilled-trades positions by 2030 (JLL, 21 April 2026). The 2.1 million is JLL's and it holds. The trillion is not \u2014 JLL attributes it to the U.S. Department of Education.\n\nBoth original sources are careful. In both cases the damage was done downstream, in the restatement.\n\nThe pattern is worth more than either number, because it is identical every time. A research house publishes a bounded finding. A secondary write-up attaches a larger number to it. After two or three citations the larger number is the one carrying the research house's name \u2014 and nobody who repeats it has opened the original.\n\nIt takes about ninety seconds to check. Open the primary source, search it for the figure. If the figure is not in there, it is not theirs.\n\nThe shortage itself is real: BLS has 504,500 plumbers employed in 2024 and roughly 44,000 openings a year through 2034. The constraint is genuine. The damage estimate is the part nobody sourced.\n\nWhat number is your own labour thesis resting on \u2014 and when did you last open the document it is attributed to?\n\n#MergersAndAcquisitions #PrivateEquity #HVAC #Plumbing #LowerMiddleMarket";

